---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/pages/work-project.css';
---

<Layout
  title="La Mer en Corps — Laurie Di Francesco"
  description="La Mer en Corps — water and dreams, giving body to movement. A year of passages."
  image="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps-cover.jpg"
>

  <!-- ── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="phero">
    <div class="phero-bg" aria-hidden="true">
      <img
        src="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps-cover.jpg"
        alt=""
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="phero-inner container">
      <p class="phero-eyebrow">Tracing</p>
      <h1>La Mer<br /><strong>en Corps</strong></h1>
      <p class="phero-caption">Water and dreams, giving body to movement.<br />A year of passages.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Vidéo (2/3) + citations Bachelard (1/3) ──────────── -->
  <div class="lmec-vid-intro rv">
    <div class="lmec-vid-col">
      <video
        autoplay
        muted
        loop
        playsinline
        preload="auto"
        poster="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps-cover.jpg"
      >
        <source src="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps/lamerenCorps.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
    </div>
    <div class="lmec-text-col">
      <div class="epi-wrap">
        <blockquote class="epi-justify">The sea is not a body one sees, nor even a body one embraces. It is a dynamic medium that responds to the dynamics of our offences.</blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="epi-wrap">
        <blockquote class="epi-justify lmec-bq-long">If the gaze of things is a little gentle, a little grave, a little pensive, it is a gaze of water. Examining imagination leads us to this paradox: in the imagination of generalised vision, water plays an unexpected part. The true eye of the earth is water. In our eyes, it is water that dreams. Are our eyes not "that unexplored pool of liquid light that God has placed deep within us"? In nature, it is still water that sees, still water that dreams.</blockquote>
        <blockquote class="epi-justify lmec-bq-short">The true eye of the earth is water. In our eyes, it is water that dreams.</blockquote>
        <cite>Gaston Bachelard — L'Eau et les Rêves <span class="epi-tradfr">freely translated from the French</span></cite>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Texte du projet — pleine largeur ─────────────────── -->
  <div class="lmec-prose rv">
    <p class="opening-eyebrow">The Project</p>
    <p class="opening-text"><em>En Corps à la Mer</em> ended where the body found water again. <em>La Mer en Corps</em> begins one year later, at the other end of that gesture: fifty-three swims along the coast, from August 2025 to July 2026.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">A body that had given way, returning to the same water until something in it changed shape. Not healing, exactly. Something closer to what a person tells themselves when they are not yet well but cannot afford to collapse: <em>nothing is final</em>. In concert, musician Benjamin Clementine stops the music and has the room repeat that line back to him, until the sentence carries the ones who carry it. Swimming works the same way. You repeat the movement to hold yourself up, and one day the movement and the water hold you up in return.</p>
    <div class="epi-wrap">
      <blockquote class="epi-justify">Repetition is not always about saying the same thing twice. Sometimes it is about returning to a wound until it changes shape.</blockquote>
      <cite>Benjamin Clementine</cite>
    </div>
    <p class="opening-text">Close to fifty thousand arm strokes. Water from fourteen degrees to twenty-seven. A heart at one hundred and thirty-seven. The year is recorded by a watch on my wrist, one point per second: latitude, longitude, temperature, pulse.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Texte avec photo flottante ──────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="lmec-prose rv">
    <figure class="lmec-photo-float">
      <img src="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps/GP010207.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <p class="opening-text">This is what interests me here. Swimming takes everything: there is no free hand, no free eye, no distance from the subject. Many times I find myself immersed in a deep blue, the exact same blue as the sky on a pure, beautiful day: my landmarks and my sense of direction vanish.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">So the recording is handed over. On one side, the most connected tools available: a satellite constellation, a sensor on my wrist, an algorithm that turns a year of positions into a drawing. On the other, the oldest and the most makeshift: a roll of film, a camera sealed inside a waterproof phone pouch, tied to the buoy. Neither gives me control. The first records without me; the second lets in salt, glare, and whatever the swell decides.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">What comes back is data, pixels, exposed silver, and most of the time blind attempts, before any of it is a photograph. I have stopped treating that blindness as a failure. It is a condition of the work: at that distance from the shore, with both arms taken, chance is not what ruins the image, it is what makes it. Turning any of this back into an image means passing through every tool that made it, from the algorithm to the developing tank. I did not want to keep any of them out of the frame.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">Superimposed, the fifty-three traces no longer describe a place. Each is brought back to its own centre; the shoreline disappears and only the gesture remains. What glows brightest is what was repeated most.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">The year closes on a loop. In music, a loop is a repeating section of sound played continuously without a noticeable break. In the sea, it looks like a figure that ends on the shore where it began. But we never come back from a swim the same: the body that finished this year is not the body that started it. Seen from the side, the loop was a climbing spiral all along.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Projet suivant ───────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="next-project container">
    <span class="next-label">Next project</span>
    <a href="/work/quando-sono-qui">Quando sono qui, non sono là →</a>
  </div>

</Layout>

<script>
  const obs = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
    entries.forEach(e => {
      if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.classList.add('in'); obs.unobserve(e.target); }
    });
  }, { threshold: .06 });
  document.querySelectorAll('.rv').forEach(el => obs.observe(el));
</script>
